course: dictionary
lesson: 817
title: Belgian: a place, a person, a word
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Belgian can work two ways. As an adjective it describes a thing: «Belgian waffles» are waffles from Belgium, a small country in western Europe. Ask yourself what the thing is and where it is from. The word Belgian tells you the place.
explain: As a noun it names a person. A Belgian is a person from Belgium, and you add -s for more than one: Belgians. Look at the words around it. If Belgian is followed by a thing, like waffles or chocolate, it is an adjective. If it is a person doing something, it is a noun.
explain: «Belgian Malinois» is a kind of dog, and the word Belgian tells you where the breed comes from. Police use these dogs to track people and search buildings. In the town of Baarle, the Belgian–Dutch border runs through streets and even houses, so the word Dutch in the sentence is a clue that a second country is nearby.
fact: Belgium is a small country in western Europe.
fact: Belgium's capital is Brussels.
fact: Belgium has three official languages: French, Dutch and German.
fact: «Belgian chocolate», «Belgian waffles» and «the Belgian coast» are all phrases that use Belgian as an adjective.
fact: «Bruxelles» is said "bru-SEL".
exercise: «Belgian waffles are sold on the seafront.» In this sentence, what kind of word is Belgian? | A verb, meaning to bake | A noun, naming a person | *An adjective, describing the waffles | A number, meaning one
exercise: What does a Belgian mean? | A kind of chocolate | A language spoken only in Germany | *A person from Belgium | A city in Belgium called Brussels
exercise: «The Belgian–Dutch border runs through streets.» Which word in the sentence shows a second country is involved? | streets | through | *Dutch | runs
test: Which is the correct plural when you mean several people from Belgium? | Belgianers | Belgiens | Belgiani | *Belgians
test: Which is NOT one of Belgium's three official languages? | Dutch | *Spanish | German | French
exam: In «Belgian chocolate», what does Belgian tell you about the chocolate? | What colour it is | How much it costs | How it tastes | *Where it comes from
